    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_f6687e94",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_id_1",
      "label": "Textual description mapped onto visual subjects",
      "description": "The text 'kids enjoying some underwater diving' is overlaid on the image of two young Black girls in shallow water. The text falsely maps the visual subjects (the girls) onto the concept of 'underwater diving,' which is not what they are doing. This creates an analogical mapping where the girls are implicitly equated with 'underwater divers' — a term that, in context, reinforces racialized stereotypes about Black people's swimming abilities. The mapping is direct: the text labels the visual entities as engaging in a specific activity (underwater diving) that they are not actually performing.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_68decdc6"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_a6b24d42",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the visual material of the two young Black girls",
      "description": "The user is not explicitly projected onto any deictic element (like 'me', 'you') in the text or visual material. However, the meme's structure invites the user to project themselves onto the scene by identifying with the subjects — the two young Black girls enjoying themselves — as the 'kids' referenced in the text. The user is implicitly invited to see themselves as part of the group being described, even though the text misrepresents their activity. This projection occurs onto the visual material (two_young_Black_girls) and the scene (girls_in_water), which are the central focus of the meme. The user is not directly addressed, but the framing of the image and text encourages identification with the subjects.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_68decdc6"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_0d0406bb",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme's text falsely claims the girls are 'underwater diving,' which misrepresents their activity and implicitly reinforces the stereotype that Black people are 'natural swimmers' or 'water people.' This stereotype, while not overtly abusive, can be part of a broader pattern of racialized assumptions about physical competence, making the meme potentially harmful in context.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_68decdc6"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
